  "cause": "**The war reached a sovereign wealth fund's headquarters on Thursday, and the kingdom's answer was that the threat had been dealt with.** A Houthi missile damaged the **Public Investment Fund Tower** in Riyadh's **King Abdullah Financial District** — an **80-storey** building, the capital's tallest, and the headquarters of Saudi Arabia's sovereign wealth fund. **Saudi civil defence** said the attack caused \"material damage\" to several floors of the tower and to nearby buildings, with **no injuries**; the statement did not name the building, but a photograph published by state news agency **SPA** showed the PIF Tower. **Reuters** reporters saw windows blown out across **seven to eight floors** and part of the metal facade bent; **three sources** told Reuters the building was struck by an incoming Houthi missile, while the **Saudi-led coalition** said it had \"dealt with a missile threat\" launched towards Riyadh and that debris had fallen. The **foreign ministry** reserved the right to take \"all necessary measures\" to defend the kingdom's sovereignty and national assets. It was the **second time in under two weeks** that a Houthi attack damaged a landmark in the same financial district — **Aramco's** building was hit on **8 November**. The explosion was heard shortly after SPA reported that **Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman** had met **CENTCOM** chief **Admiral Brad Cooper** in Riyadh. **Overnight into Friday the exchange did not pause, and both sides described an expanding map.** The coalition said it intercepted a **ballistic missile** aimed at the kingdom's southern region and drones over **Jazan, Abha and Khamis Mushait**, where **King Khalid Air Base** sits; the **Houthis** said the kingdom intercepted **five drones and one ballistic missile** on Friday morning. Iran's own signalling is explicit about scale: IRGC Aerospace Force adviser **Brigadier General Ali Belali** told **IRNA** that there are \"no limits\" on Iran's missiles, including use in **\"new geographical areas\"** if ordered; the commander of the **Khatam al-Anbiya** headquarters, **Brigadier General Abdolreza Abed**, told **Tasnim**: *\"If they hit infrastructure, we will certainly hit their infrastructure.\"* Behind the exchange sits a **US naval blockade** in force since **13 April** and resumed on **14 July** at the commander-in-chief's direction — CENTCOM reports **130 vessels redirected** and **three ships disabled** in the current phase, the latest a **Panama-flagged bulk carrier** that tried to run it, while an internal **MARAD** memo obtained by **Al Jazeera** warned that the blockade was raising Gulf insurance premiums and cutting cargo capacity. In **Iraq**, security forces deployed to **Jurf al-Sakhar** — a **Kataib Hezbollah** stronghold off-limits to the state since **2014** — after two months of negotiations, on the orders of the commander-in-chief.",
