  "verse": "Do not stand on the side of evil and unfairness (irresponsibility), of unrighteousness (consciencelessness) and of all terrible things, rather stand on the side of right so that you do not fall into the depths of an abyss (of unright); if unclearness and dissensions (tensions/strifes) arise between people of your kind (fellow human beings) or between your peoples, then search for a mutual arrangement so that you may consider (clarify) your conflict (altercation) and unfavour (enmity) and create peace amongst one another; and bring about such arrangements for consideration (clarification) of the situation before it is too late and your true discernment (rationality) turns into undiscernment (irrationality); determine through your agreements that peace and freedom as well as consonance (harmony) shall prevail rather than battle (war) being considered…",
  "verseSrc": "Chapter 8, Verse 43",
  "hinge": "§8:43 does not ask that differences be dissolved — it asks that they be **arranged**: \"search for a mutual arrangement,\" and \"determine through your agreements that peace and freedom… shall prevail.\" An agreement that arranges a difference is exactly what a reset summit is for, and the verse's warning is about timing: bring the arrangement about *before* rationality turns into irrationality — a description of what a decade of Brexit argument has done to the ability of either side to hear the other's numbers. The hinge is a change in **what the agreement is made accountable to**. **First,** publish an **alignment annex** listing every EU rule the UK would adopt and the domestic statute each displaces, because rule-taking that is not inventoried becomes an argument about identity instead of cost — and an argument about identity has no negotiating text. **Second,** put the package to a **substantive vote with a white paper**, not merely a majority of one: a regime built on continuous alignment needs more consent than one division provides, and the durability discount Brussels applies is a direct function of how thin that consent looks. **Third,** the carbon link should carry a **review clause tied to the border adjustment**, so the clause that is hardest to leave is at least the clause that was argued in public. §8:43's standard is not union; it is arrangement — an agreement explicit enough that neither side can later claim it was never told what it was signing."
